Some ideas run through the whole project. Others belong to one module and are
explained where that module lives. This page gathers the project-wide ones, the
vocabulary and the contracts you carry into every layer. It reads in order: what
a capability is, how you reach a peer in the first place, what the conversation
looks like on the wire, and the memory contract that keeps half a million of
those conversations cheap.

## Capabilities, CapTP, and OCapN

A capability is just a reference you cannot fake. If you hold one, you can call the
object it points at, and if you do not, there is no name to guess and no flag to
flip. Authority travels by passing the reference itself, which is why the three
moves that matter all fall out for free. You delegate by handing the reference along,
you attenuate by handing along a narrower wrapper instead, and you revoke by dropping
the wrapper so the calls stop landing.

[A capability is a reference, not a copy. Pass it as an argument and the partner can call it; if you never receive it, there is nothing to forge.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-capability.svg'))

That picture is static. It says who is allowed to call whom. A call has a shape of
its own. You send it and hold a promise the same instant, without waiting for the
partner to do the work. The partner's later resolve settles that promise into a
result.

[A single call, start to finish: you deliver it and immediately hold a promise, and the partner's resolve settles that promise to a result.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-call.svg'))

If this is new, start at the beginning.
[Capabilities, CapTP, and OCapN, from zero](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/capabilities.md)
explains what a capability is and why this beats ambient authority, and what
[CapTP](http://erights.org/elib/distrib/captp/) adds over a plain socket, an idea
that traces back to the [E language](http://erights.org/). All of that comes
before any pancake API appears. It also introduces [Spritely Goblins](https://spritely.institute/goblins/)
and the [OCapN community](https://github.com/ocapn/ocapn), which together are the
conformance authority everything here is measured against.

## Locators: reaching a peer in the first place

A capability is a reference you already hold, which leaves one fair question: how do
you get the *first* one, from a peer you have never met? You cannot forge a
reference, so that is not the way in. The way in is a **locator**, and locators are
a pillar of OCapN in their own right, alongside the wire protocol and the value
model.

A locator is an *address*, not a reference: the thing an `ocapn://` URI writes
down, the text you paste into a config or share out of band. It carries three
parts, spelled `ocapn://<designator>.<transport>`: a designator that names the
peer, a transport symbol (`tcp`, `iroh`) that says how to reach it, and any hints
needed to connect.

Using one is two moves. Open a session per the locator, then fetch a published
capability from the peer's *bootstrap object*. That is your first live reference,
and from there you are back in capability-land, where references travel by being
passed and no one needs a locator again.

The **hints** are `?key=value` pairs on the end of the URI: extra routing
information for transports that need it. iroh's direct posture needs address hints
to route a node id; its discovery postures need none. Either way a hint is only a
routing aid, never authority: a missing hint can stop you from *reaching* a peer,
but no hint can *forge* one.

A **sturdyref** is the long-lived form: a locator plus a *swiss number*, an
unguessable token naming one object at that peer. It survives a restart, because it
carries no live session state. Redeeming it is the same two moves as any first
contact, and what returns is an ordinary reference.

[Everything left of the hints is an address, and none of it is authority. Only the swiss number names something you can be given.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-locator.svg'))

The [protocol cheatsheet]($link.page('protocol')) names the two locator records, the
[Transports]($link.page('transports')) page covers the transports a locator can
name, and the
[sturdyref and locator diagrams](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/protocol-diagrams/07-sturdyrefs-and-locators.md)
draw the enlivenment flow.

> **Swiss number**: the unguessable secret a sturdyref carries to name one specific
> object. There is no list to check and no login: holding the string *is* the
> permission, and not holding it leaves nothing to guess. The name comes from the
> numbered Swiss bank account, where the number alone is the access.

## The conversations on the wire

[The Spritely Goblins mascot, sizing up a stack of pancakes. pancake is checked against a real Goblins peer, so the two have to agree on the wire.]($image.siteAsset('pancake-goblin-512.png'))

A protocol is easiest to trust when you can see it. The
[protocol diagrams](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/protocol-diagrams/README.md)
draw the CapTP conversations pancake is built to honor: the session lifecycle,
deliver and resolve, promises and pipelining, references and tables, garbage
collection, third-party handoffs, and sturdyrefs. They are the picture the code is
checked against.

> **Promise pipelining**: when you call a remote object you get a *promise* for the
> answer right away, and you may call methods on that promise before it has resolved.
> Each follow-on call is forwarded to wherever the answer turns out to live, so a
> chain of dependent calls makes one round trip instead of one per step.

> **Three-party handoff**: the move that lets one peer introduce you to another. If a
> peer you trust holds a reference to a third party and wants to pass it to you, CapTP
> carries a signed introduction you present to that third party yourself. From then on
> you call them directly, rather than relaying every call through the peer in the middle.
> In the application surface a multi-session CapTP host presents that introduction
> yourself: a Host + `driver.Bridge` call settles directly with the live reference;
> [example chapter 02](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/zig/02_introduce_and_redeem.zig)
> walks the whole move.

### Codecs: the seam beneath the conversation

Those messages still have to be written down as bytes, and OCapN settles how: the
format is [Syrup](https://github.com/ocapn/syrup). But Syrup is a *codec*, a way to
write a message down, not the protocol itself. The session deals in messages
and never in bytes; a separate codec layer does the writing, and the session never
learns which codec did the work.

That separation is what lets pancake carry a second codec,
[Cap'n Proto](https://capnproto.org/), and even one you bring yourself. The
[Codecs]($link.page('codecs')) page lays out which codecs ship, how they trade
off, and how to add another.

### CapTP versions, and who is on the other end

Picking a codec is only half of it. Two peers also settle on a *version* of the
protocol, by negotiation rather than luck. Each peer brings a **menu**: the
versions, codecs, and optional features it can speak, best first. Both ends run
the same rule: keep what the two menus share, most-preferred first. Both reach
the identical pick with no haggling and no extra round trip.

[Both peers run the same rule over the two menus and reach the same pick.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-negotiation.svg'))

Each side just needs to see the other's menu. In one process that is free: both
menus are in hand. Over a network no menu travels; each end's version and codec
are configuration, and the default on both sides is the interop floor every OCapN
implementation shares. Only then does `op:start-session` go on the wire, carrying
the agreed version string. The two strings line up because both sides computed the
same answer. If the menus share no version or codec, the session aborts rather
than guess.

That is why pancake speaks two dialects, depending on who answers.

[The dialect depends on who answers: the canonical floor against Goblins, pancake's own profile between two pancake peers.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-dialects.svg'))

Against Spritely Goblins, the shared menu is the floor: CapTP **1.0** over Syrup,
exactly what the canonical implementation speaks. That is the interop anchor: if
pancake and Goblins disagree, pancake is wrong. Against another pancake peer,
the menu reaches higher: the `pancake-pre-spec` profile and the Cap'n Proto codec.
The application code is identical either way; only the bytes on the wire change.

## Frames, ownership, and the memory contract

Whichever codec writes the conversation down, the bytes it produces need an
owner. pancake treats every allocation and copy on the wire path as a design event. Each one
is forbidden, named, or measured, and never left to chance. The rule that makes this
tractable is an explicit split in who owns the bytes: a `Frame` owns its wire bytes, a
`FrameView` borrows them for a bounded scope, and session state never holds on to
borrowed memory.

The budget that falls out is two numbers. A steady-state delivery to a live exported
object costs exactly **one heap allocation**: the owned-frame copy the event model requires,
and nothing more. And a whole warmed connection, one that has already processed a
delivery, holds **under about two kilobytes** of protocol state. A build gate holds
both, so they stay facts rather than hopes. The
[architecture](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/architecture.md)
spells out the full memory and copy contract.

## Built for half a million connections on one node

That budget serves one target: a single ordinary server holding about **500,000**
live OCapN connections. At that scale memory gives out long before CPU does, so
the per-connection numbers above are the whole game: two kilobytes across 500k is
well under a gigabyte of core state.

Half a million is a design target, not a count clocked on a live wire. It is large
enough to force a sans-io core and sub-two-kilobyte sessions, and small enough to
fit on one tuned machine if the budget holds. Above it the limits are the
kernel's, which pancake has not measured yet.

The [ladder](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/ladder.md) has the
exact ceilings and the arithmetic; the [Performance]($link.page('performance')) page has
the benchmarks and how to run them.

## What proves it

Every semantic claim in these docs is backed by something that runs. Three proof layers do
the work:

- **Live Goblins interop.** pancake holds a real, two-way OCapN conversation with a
  Spritely Goblins peer, Goblins calling pancake and pancake calling back, from every
  binding and not just from Zig.
- **The upstream [OCapN test suite](https://github.com/ocapn/ocapn-test-suite)**,
  run against a live pancake peer.
- **Cross-codec replay.** The same conversation runs through Syrup and Cap'n Proto and
  must produce an identical trace, so switching codecs cannot quietly change behavior.

The [project page]($link.page('project')) tracks how much of the protocol these cover
today, and the [examples]($link.page('examples')) let you watch a round-trip run.
